      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Excretion – Concept and Importance"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Excretion is the biological process of removing metabolic nitrogenous wastes such as ammonia, urea, and uric acid from the body.",
        "The human excretory system comprises a pair of kidneys, a pair of ureters, a urinary bladder, and a single urethra.",
        "The nephron is the structural and functional unit of the kidney, consisting of the Bowman's capsule, Glomerulus, PCT, Loop of Henle, and DCT.",
        "Urine formation occurs in three sequential steps: Glomerular Ultrafiltration, Selective Reabsorption, and Tubular Secretion.",
        "Hormones like ADH (Vasopressin) and Aldosterone regulate water reabsorption and blood pressure through the RAAS mechanism."
      ],
      "id": "t984z35"
    },
